nds were an end to mandatory  arbitration causes and contracts  reforming the hiring and promotion  policies to improve representation at  all levels  publishing data on relative compensation  to ensure fair payment  and to create a company-wide dei task  force that can hire a third party to  audit activision blizzard's hr and  executive staff  plenty of folks in the games industry  showed their support for the workers who  walked out and demonstrated and even  ubisoft employees published a letter in  support of them demanding fair treatment  across the entire industry  as reported by axios reporter stephen  tottillo so on july  28th in response to the walkout  activision blizzard ceo bobby kotick  made a statement in which he promised  things were going to change at the  company but the very next day activision  blizzard hired the law firm wilmer hale  to review their hr policies which as  kotaku's mike fahey pointed out  was most recently in the news for their  work trying to prevent amazon employees  from unionizing  now while it is pretty common to have an  outside firm review hr policies when you  have a situation like this  a lot of people saw this move as a slap  in the face to concerned employees both  because of wilmer hale's history of  union busting  and also because the employees weren't  consulted about this hiring at all  to be clear a move like this can be made  with the best of intentions  but when the party being sued is hiring  lawyers for mediation and review  without consulting the people who wanted  that mediation review  you can see how it looks pretty [ __ ]  bad on august 3rd activision blizzard  employees who have organized calling  themselves the abk workers alliance sent  out another letter in response to both  codex comments and to the hiring of  wilmer hale  the letter states that activision  blizzard employees didn't find that  kodik's response addressed their  concerns  it also says that the hiring of loma  hale is both a conflict of interest and  doesn't represent the employee's best  interests  in response to our demands you wrote a  letter to employees expressing a  commitment to doing a better job of  listening  you said you would do everything  possible to work with employees in  improving our workplace  and yet the solutions you proposed in  that letter did not meaningfully address  our requests  you ignored our call for an end to  mandatory arbitration you did not commit  to adopting inclusive recruitment and  hiring practices  you made no comment on pay transparency  essentially activision blizzard  employees feel that not only is the  company not listening to their demands  they're  actively working against them and then  just a few hours after that letter was  published blizzard president j  alan brack stepped down from his role at  the company brack is being replaced by  jen o'neill and mike ibarra who will be  the co-heads of blizzard going forward  in a statement brac said i am confident  that jen o'neil and mike ibarra will  provide the leadership blizzard needs to  realize its full potential  and will accelerate the pace of change i  anticipate they will do so with passion  and enthusiasm and that they can be  trusted to lead with the highest level  of integrity and commitment to the  components of our culture that make  blizzard so special  brac leaving is a huge deal he was with  the company for a long time since 2005  and has been president since mike  morheim left in 2018  but many have criticized brac's  departure by suggesting that it's  something of a smokescreen  while brac may or may not have been  directly responsible for the culture of  harassment and discrimination at the  company  activision blizzard employees never  asked for his or anyone else's  resignation  all they wanted was more transparency  and a seat at the table when it came to  discussing inclusion and safety in the  workplace but brac was not the only  person who left blizzard this week  on august 3rd bloomberg's jason schreier  reported that jesse messchuck blizzard's  senior people officer and top hr rep  left the company as well  this departure wasn't seen as terribly  surprising given the allegations in the  lawsuit that hr wasn't trusted within  the company and a follow-up report from  axios's meghan faraok manesh about how  hr would defend known abusers and did  not keep reports confidential  farooqkamesh's report was far from the  only one to come out following the  lawsuit there has been a lot of  reporting in the last couple of days  that are uncovering some pretty  disturbing stories from activision  blizzard  vice's lorenzo francischi bcri reported  on the time a blizzard employee at a  recruiting conference asked a woman  about the last time she was penetrated  and kotaku's ethan gatch reported on the  truth behind the cosby suite  a hotel room used by blizzard employees  during blizzcon to drink a network which  featured a literal photo of bill cosby  as photos posted by former blizzard  employees  and obtained by kotaku show the lawsuit  alleges that the cosby suite was former  wow senior creative director alex of  frasier's room at blizzcon  and since he was allegedly a known  abuser and harasser  his room was nicknamed the cosby suite  after alleged rapist bill cosby  since kotaku's report came out former  blizzard developer and kurt ryan  employee greg street has alleged that  the cosby suite was a break room and  that was nicknamed as such because of an  inside joke  and that he is now embarrassed by the  nickname kotaku also reported that  activision blizzard's executive vice  president and former george w bush  administration member fran townsend  went on a twitter blocking spree after  she was criticized for insensitive  comments following the lawsuit deceros  theo salon reported that t-mobile has  seemingly pulled out of their overwatch  league and call of duty league  sponsorships  while neither t-mobile or blizzard made  any statements it's not hard to imagine  that this could have been a result of  the lawsuit  then on august 3rd activision blizzard  had their quarterly investor relations  call which was criticized by basically  everybody listening as being  extraordinarily tone deaf as it focused  more on upcoming game launches than  addressing any of these alleged issues  at the company  and finally also on august 3 activision  blizzard shareholders filed a class  action lawsuit against activision  blizzard  for not disclosing information about  harassment complaints at the company and  investigations into the company with  regards to discrimination  of course don't get too excited about  the shareholders coming to save the day  they mostly only care about the money as  rs technica's kyle orland points out the  shareholders are concerned because  withholding this info led to a quote  artificially inflated share price  basically activision blizzard stock  dropped from 90  to 84 on the day the news of the lawsuit  broke and has dropped as low as 79  in the day since so activision blizzard  shareholders feel like they're entitled  to damages because they think the  company lied to them about how stable  the company was  which brings us to today because this  story is far from over  activision blizzard employees demands  have still not been met activision  blizzard is hiring a union-busting law  firm to run damage control  and now we have two lawsuits to settle  but here  while we have a lull in the action i  want to take a moment to say that the  mountain of allegations about the  culture at activision blizzard and the  behavior of the staff of the company is  overwhelming i'd like to think that it's  not controversial to say that nobody  should ever be made to feel unsafe at  work  and that nobody should ever be paid less  because of their gender or the color of  their skin  my point is that the activision blizzard  employee's demands are  reasonable and i really do hope  activision blizzard listens because  i want to see some real reform in the  games industry unfortunately given  activision blizzard's response over the  last week i find that really unlikely  instead i think this story is just gonna  keep going at least until activision  blizzard loses a lawsuit or has to pay  out the victims  [Music]  you

